        "heading": "3.4 FIELD PROGRAMMABLE GATE ARRAY (FPGA)",
        "text": "Field programmable gate arrays (FPGAs) are general-purpose, multilevel semiconductor devices that are built around a matrix of configurable logic blocks (CLBs) connected via programmable interconnects. The programmable interconnects allow the end users to program FPGAs to suit their needs (Brown et al., 1992; Xilinx). The ability of FPGAs to change their functionality after design gives them an edge over Application Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC). The FPGAs also use a smaller board space and can be more energy efficient than the equivalent discrete circuit (DSL-Ltd, 2021). State of the art FPGAs possess high processing capabilities and they can be used for data processing and machine learning at the edge. The following are use cases of FPGA in data collection and processing and machine learning tasks."
